<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/advanced/language-and-meaning">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Philosophy of language is <strong>20th-century philosophy's engine room</strong>: most major
    figures (Frege, Russell, Wittgenstein, Quine, Kripke, Davidson) built their systems on how
    words mean. The stakes are real — meaning, reference, truth and communication are the
    machinery under law, AI language models and every argument ever held.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="sense, reference, use — and the speech acts hiding in plain speech" widget="equationStep" config={langCfg} />

  <h2>Names, necessity and Kripke</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The causal theory of names (Kripke, 1972):</strong> names refer by a historical chain to their bearer, not by description in the speaker's head. "Aristotle" refers even if all our beliefs about him are wrong — refuting descriptivism and reviving metaphysics.</li>
    <li><strong>Necessary a posteriori:</strong> "water is H₂O" is discovered by science yet true in every possible world — a category Kripke made respectable, reshaping philosophy of science and mind.</li>
    <li><strong>Quine's challenge:</strong> "Two dogmas of Empiricism" (1951) attacked the analytic/synthetic distinction itself — meaning became holism (beliefs confirmed as webs). The most destabilising paper of the century.</li>
    <li><strong>Grice and implicature:</strong> "Can you pass the salt?" is a request, not a question — conversation runs on cooperative maxims. Pragmatics (the linguistics ladder) grew from exactly this observation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Frege (1892); Russell (1905); Wittgenstein (1921, 1953); Kripke, Naming and Necessity (1972); Austin, How to Do Things with Words (1962).</p>
</TopicPage>
